=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Describe evolution as changes in allele frequencies.
- Compare mutation, gene flow, selection, and drift.
- Explain how migration changes a population gene pool.
- Trace allele changes under the mutation examples.

=== layout: section

# From this point forward, no more equations will appear on the exam. Only definitions.

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Genetics and Evolution*

@body

- Evolution consists of progressive changes in the gene pool
  - progressive adaptation of a population to its  environment
  - changes in the allele frequency
- Four processes account for most changes in allele frequency\:
  - Mutation
  - Migration/Gene flow
  - Natural Selection
  - Random Genetic Drift
- Only natural selection causes adaptive evolution

=== layout: one-panel

# *Genetic Drift*

@body

- The smaller a sample, the greater the chance of random deviation from a predicted result
  - Genetic drift describes how allele frequencies fluctuate unpredictably from one generation to the next
- Genetic drift tends to *reduce genetic variation* through losses of alleles

=== layout: one-panel

# *Natural Selection*

@body

- Evolution by natural selection involves both chance and &#8220;sorting&#8221;
  - New genetic variations arise by chance
  - Beneficial alleles are &#8220;sorted&#8221; and favored by natural selection
- Only natural selection consistently increases the frequencies of alleles that provide *reproductive advantage*

=== layout: one-panel

# *Gene Flow/Migration*

@body

- Gene flow consists of the movement of alleles among populations
  - Alleles can be transferred through the movement of fertile individuals or gametes (for example, pollen)
- Gene flow tends to *reduce genetic variation* among populations over time

=== layout: two-panels

# *Migration*

@left

- The introduction of genes by recent migrations can alter allele and genotype frequencies within a population and disrupt Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium.

@right

![Slide image 1](assets/lect15b-genetic_evolution/image_001.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# *Merged Populations*

@body

- The merger of two randomly mating populations does not produce a population with Hardy-Weinberg genotype frequencies.
- However, if the merged population mates randomly for one generation, Hardy-Weinberg genotype frequencies will be established.

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme


@body

- Migration rates essentially work like Mutation rates, so we can treat them as one.
- So, let&#8217;s learn about mutations...

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Mutation*

@body

- Ultimate source of genetic variation &#8211; however the rate of mutation is low and harmful mutations are eliminated by natural selection.
- Low mutation rates are thought to be evolved as a compromise.
  - What do we mean by this?

=== layout: one-panel

@body

Irreversible Mutation [A]{color=red}&#8594;[a]{color=#800080}

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Irreversible Mutation*

@body

- Consider two alleles\: [*A*]{color=red} and [*a*]{color=#800080}, suppose [*A*]{color=red} undergoes mutation to [*a*]{color=#800080} ([A]{color=red}&#8594;[a]{color=#800080}) at a rate [*&#181;*]{color=blue} per generation
  - Proportion of [*A*]{color=red} allele that do not mutate is 1-[&#181;]{color=blue}
- If we let [p]{color=#00A933} and q represent frequencies of [*A*]{color=red} and [*a*]{color=#800080}
  - The current n-th generation has the frequency, [pn]{color=#00A933}
- Allele frequency of [*A*]{color=red} in the next (n+1)-th generation represents\:
  - [pn]{color=#00A933} = [pn-1]{color=#00A933} (1-[&#181;]{color=blue})
  - Frequency of allele in the previous generation times proportion of alleles that failed to mutate in that generation.

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Irreversible Mutation*

@body

- [*A*]{color=red} undergoes mutation to [*a*]{color=purple} ([A]{color=red}&#8594;[a]{color=purple}) at a rate [*&#181;*]{color=blue} per generation
- [p0]{color=#00A933} is the frequency of [*A*]{color=red} in the original generation
  - Allele frequency of [A]{color=red} in the n-th generation represents\:
  - [pn]{color=#00A933} = [p0]{color=#00A933} (1-[&#181;]{color=blue})n
- Eventually, the generations increase p goes to zero.
  - [p&#8734;]{color=#00A933} = [p0]{color=#00A933} (1-[&#181;]{color=blue})&#8734; &#8594; 0
- At equilibrium, the population is then 100% allele [a]{color=purple}.
  - this is probably not realistic...

=== layout: one-panel

@body

Reversible Mutations and Equilibrium [A]{color=red}&#8594;[a]{color=#800080} and [a]{color=#800080}&#8594;[A]{color=red}

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Reversible Mutation*

@body

- Forward mutation of [A]{color=red} &#8594; [a]{color=purple} is offset by reverse mutation of [a]{color=purple} &#8594; [A]{color=red}.
  - Rate of [forward]{color=blue} mutation ([A]{color=red}&#8594;[a)]{color=purple} is [&#181;]{color=blue}
  - Rate of [reverse]{color=#EA7500} mutation ([a]{color=purple}&#8594;[A]{color=red}) is [*&#957;*]{color=#EA7500} (greek nu)
- At equilibrium (allele frequency becomes constant)&#8230; no changes in the allele frequency
- Equilibrium value of [p]{color=#00A933} is denoted with a circumflex (carrot hat)\: [&#710;p]{color=#00A933}.

![Slide image 2](assets/lect15b-genetic_evolution/image_002.svg)

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Effect of mutation on allele frequency*

@body

![Slide image 3](assets/lect15b-genetic_evolution/image_003.jpg)

- Allele frequency is changed very slowly by mutation. In the case of reversible mutation, an equilibrium state is reached where the allele frequency becomes constant.

